.TH "ST\-XCLIP" "1" "January 2020" "secure-terminal" "secure-terminal Manual"
.SH "NAME"
\fBst\-xclip\fR \- xclip that sanitizes a clipboard write
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBst\-xclip\fR [\fIXCLIP\-OPTION\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
st\-xclip is a drop\-in replacement for \fBxclip\fR(1) (X11) that filters standard input through \fBsclip\fR(1) on a clipboard WRITE\. Every argument is forwarded verbatim to \fBxclip\fR, so native options such as \fB\-selection clipboard\fR work unchanged\.
.P
xclip is one command for both directions, so st\-xclip uses a simple, honest rule rather than parsing xclip's option grammar:
.IP "\(bu" 4
a READ (\fB\-o\fR / \fB\-out\fR), or \fB\-help\fR / \fB\-version\fR, is passed through unchanged;
.IP "\(bu" 4
a piped standard input that is not a read is sanitized through \fBsclip\fR;
.IP "\(bu" 4
anything else (standard input is a terminal: a file\-operand write, or an interactive invocation) is passed through unchanged\.
.IP "" 0
.P
So it sanitizes the common \fBecho \|\.\|\.\|\. | st\-xclip \-selection clipboard\fR form\. For a file\-operand write (\fBxclip file\fR) use the raw pipeline instead:
.IP "" 4
.nf
sclip < file | xclip \-selection clipboard
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
By default the strip is strictest (printable ASCII only)\. Set the environment variable \fBSCLIP_MODE=unicode\fR to keep printable non\-ASCII instead\.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
\fBSCLIP_MODE\fR
When set to \fBunicode\fR, keep printable non\-ASCII (passes \fB\-\-unicode\fR to \fBsclip\fR)\. Any other value (or unset) is the default ASCII\-only strip\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The exit status of \fBxclip\fR, or of the \fBsclip\fR | \fBxclip\fR pipeline on a sanitized write; 127 if \fBxclip\fR is not installed\.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBst\-wl\-paste\fR(1), \fBst\-wl\-copy\fR(1), \fBsclip\fR(1), \fBxclip\fR(1)
